.\" Manual page for doorman, the Call Me Maybe lobby daemon.
.\" Render locally with:  man ./docs/doorman.1
.TH DOORMAN 1 "2026-07-30" "Call Me Maybe" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
doorman \- lobby daemon and operations tool for a Call Me Maybe home phone
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B doorman
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.PP
.B doorman
.RI "# with no subcommand, runs the service"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B doorman
decides who gets connected. Asterisk handles SIP, RTP, and DTMF detection;
.B doorman
talks to it over ARI on loopback and applies policy. One call is one
goroutine, and the caller hanging up cancels a context that unwinds
everything.
.PP
Known callers hear a welcome prompt and ring the whole house. Everyone else
meets the lobby: dial a 6\-digit extension within the dial window, or the
bouncer says "Good day" and hangs up.
.PP
It is also its own operations tool. Every subcommand below runs against the
same configuration and the same validator the daemon uses, so what
.B doorman check
accepts is exactly what the daemon will accept.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B doorman
Run the service. Configuration comes from the environment; see
.B ENVIRONMENT
below and
.IR examples/.env.example .
Exits non\-zero if ARI is unreachable at startup, which is deliberate: a
lobby daemon that cannot answer calls should fail loudly.
.TP
.BI "doorman check [\-handsets " path "] [" path ]
Validate
.I policy.toml
and
.I handsets.toml
together and report what they add up to \(em allow\-list size, extension
count, PIN length, the house ring group, and then every extension with each
of its settings spelled out, including the ones left at their default. A
setting shown as
.RB \(lq (none\ \(em\ ...) \(rq
resolved to nothing, which is how a misspelled optional key becomes visible:
you configured quiet hours and the output says there are none. PINs are shown
only as their length, never their value.
.IP
A key that matches no field in the schema is an error here, and only here.
The daemon warns about the same key and carries on, because a policy it
loaded yesterday must not start taking the phone down today; this command is
a linting tool with an operator reading its output, where silence about a
typo is the whole defect. Suggestions are offered \(em
.B afterhous
is told about
.BR afterhours .
Exits 0 when valid, 1 when not, naming the offending key.
.IP
On a box answering several numbers this reports every line it found \(em its
name, its file, and whether it loads \(em before describing each in turn, and
notes a PIN that appears on more than one line. It says nothing about lines at
all when there is only the default one.
This is the authority on whether a configuration is usable.
.TP
.BI "doorman schema [" name ]
Print the configuration surface as JSON Schema (draft 2020\-12) on stdout:
every key, type, pattern, default, and cross\-file reference for
.IR policy.toml ,
.IR handsets.toml ,
and the environment.
.I name
is
.BR policy ,
.BR handsets ,
or
.BR env ;
all three are emitted as one bundle when omitted.
.IP
This exists for tooling and for language models. It describes
.IR shape ;
it is not a validity oracle. JSON Schema cannot express cross\-file
identifier references, mutually exclusive keys, or the roughly thirty
semantic rules the validator applies \(em those appear as
.B x\-cross\-references
and
.B x\-rules
annotations, and the document says so itself. Run
.B doorman check
before relying on a config. See also
.I llms.txt
in the repository root.
.TP
.BI "doorman rotate [\-policy " path "] [\-handsets " path "] [" label ...]
Generate new extension PINs with
.IR crypto/rand ,
rewriting
.I policy.toml
in place with comments intact. Rotates every extension when no labels are
given, or only the named ones. A running daemon picks the change up within a
second. New PINs are printed to stdout \(em the one place they are ever
shown \(em and are never written to the logs.
.TP
.BI "doorman render [\-handsets " path "] [\-out " dir "] [\-env " path ]
Generate the per\-handset Asterisk configuration from
.IR handsets.toml :
PJSIP endpoints, internal numbers, BLF hints, and the ring\-all and page\-all
groups. This makes the inventory authoritative over the dialplan so the two
cannot drift. Handset SIP passwords are read from the environment or
.I .env
via each handset's
.B password_env
key; the generated file contains real secrets and must be treated as such.
.TP
.B doorman lsp
Run a language server over stdio for
.I policy.toml
and
.IR handsets.toml .
Diagnostics come from the same validator that guards the daemon, so unknown
handset ids, bad schedule references, and duplicate PINs are underlined as
you type, with completions for every cross\-reference. Works in Neovim over
SSH on the Pi with no additional installation. See
.IR docs/editor.md .
.TP
.B doorman calls
.RS
Read the call log: one line per completed call, with who called, what happened
and why. Caller IDs are redacted unless
.BR \-\-no\-redact ,
so the default output is safe to paste into a bug report or hand to a model.
Entered digits and PINs are never recorded at all \(em a record says whether a
PIN was valid, never what it was. Requires
.B CALL_LOG_PATH
to be set; see
.BR "doorman schema env" .
.PP
Filters:
.BR \-\-since " (a duration like " 24h " or " 7d ", or a date), "
.BR \-\-outcome ", " \-\-caller ", " \-n .
.B \-\-json
emits JSON Lines.
.RE
.PP
.BI "doorman e164 " number
Show how a raw caller ID normalises to E.164. Use this when a known caller
unexpectedly meets the bouncer: the carrier may be handing over a format
that does not match what is written in the allow\-list.
.TP
.B doorman version
Print the version. A release binary reports its git tag.
.TP
.B doorman help
Print a usage summary.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I policy.toml
The rules: allow\-list, extensions, ringer ladders, schedules. Changes
weekly and is safe to hand\-edit \(em when
.B POLICY_WATCH
is enabled, edits go live without a restart, and a file that fails
validation is logged and discarded while the last good policy stays in
service. Contains PINs; keep it mode 0600.
.TP
.I handsets.toml
The hardware inventory: what phones exist, their ids, endpoints, internal
numbers, and which
.I .env
variable holds each SIP password. Changes when hardware is bought.
.TP
.I .env
Secrets and tuning. Loaded by systemd's
.B EnvironmentFile
in production. Contains the ARI password and every handset password; keep it
mode 0600.
.TP
.I examples/
Templates to copy for all three of the above.
.TP
.I /var/lib/asterisk/sounds/<prefix>/
Pre\-rendered prompt audio, where
.I <prefix>
is
.BR PROMPT_MEDIA_PREFIX .
Prompts are built once with piper on a workstation and committed as WAVs, so
the Pi never synthesises speech. Pointing the prefix at another directory is
how a prompt pack is swapped in.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B doorman schema env
prints all of these with types, defaults, and explanations. The frequently
relevant ones:
.TP
.BR ARI_USERNAME ", " ARI_PASSWORD
Required. Must match
.IR asterisk/ari.conf .
.TP
.B ARI_BASE_URL
Default
.IR http://127.0.0.1:8088 .
Keep ARI bound to loopback.
.TP
.B ARI_APP
Default
.IR doorman .
Must match the
.B Stasis()
application named in
.IR asterisk/extensions.conf .
.TP
.BR POLICY_PATH ", " HANDSETS_PATH ", " POLICY_WATCH
Where the config files are, and whether to reload them on change.
.B POLICY_PATH
also anchors the search for extra lines: any
.I policy.<line>.toml
beside it configures the line of that name, which the dialplan selects with
.BR "Stasis(app,line,<name>)" .
Each line gets its own store, so one bad file cannot take down another line.
Bare
.I policy.toml
is the default line and serves every call that names none.
A new line file is picked up at startup; edits to an existing one are live.
.TP
.B FIRST_DIGIT_TIMEOUT_MS
Default 10000. Time to dial the
.I first
digit, measured from the end of the greeting; a stranger reading a PIN off a
card needs longer than a stopwatch allows.
.B INTER_DIGIT_TIMEOUT_MS
(default 3000) governs the gap between subsequent digits.
.TP
.BR RATELIMIT_ENABLED ", " RATELIMIT_MAX_FAILURES ", " RATELIMIT_WINDOW_MS
After repeated failures, a caller skips the greeting entirely and goes
straight to dismissal, leaving nothing to brute\-force against.
.TP
.BR LOG_LEVEL ", " LOG_FORMAT ", " LOG_REDACT_CALLER_ID
.B trace|debug|info|warn|error
,
.B json|pretty
, and whether to log only the last four digits of unknown callers.
.TP
.BR WEBHOOK_URL ", " WEBHOOK_REDACT_CALLER_ID
Unset by default. With a URL,
.B doorman
POSTs one JSON event when the house starts ringing and one when a call ends,
so Home Assistant can announce the caller or flash a light. The payload names
no speaker and no routing \(em Home Assistant decides who hears it. Delivery
is off the call path and can never delay a call. Caller IDs are redacted
unless the second variable is set false; entered digits and PINs are never
sent at any setting. Treat the URL as a credential: a Home Assistant webhook
id is the whole secret, and
.B doorman
logs only its host.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed \(em an invalid configuration, an unreachable ARI, an
unwritable output directory.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong.
.SH EXAMPLES
Validate a configuration before trusting it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
doorman check \-\-handsets examples/handsets.example.toml \\
              examples/policy.example.toml
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give a language model the whole configuration surface:
.PP
.RS
.EX
doorman schema > /tmp/doorman\-schema.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Work out why a known caller met the bouncer:
.PP
.RS
.EX
doorman e164 "(512) 555\-0100"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Rotate one extension's PIN after it has been shared too widely:
.PP
.RS
.EX
doorman rotate Kids
.EE
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Extensions are credentials. A 6\-digit PIN reachable from the PSTN is a
million combinations \(em ample against a person, thin against a machine that
can redial \(em which is why the rate limiter exists and why
.B doorman rotate
generates PINs rather than letting you choose them.
.PP
Caller IDs and PINs never appear in the logs. That invariant is enforced
mechanically by a static analysis pass in the repository
.RI ( tools/nologsecrets ),
not by convention: logging a digit
.I count
is allowed, logging the digits is not.
.PP
Withheld caller ID always meets the bouncer, and anonymous callers share a
single rate\-limit bucket. One persistent blocked caller therefore makes the
lobby stricter for every other withheld number. That is the intended trade.
